he faith the first visit of  the pope uh  to you know a country that has been to  some degree  uh in political isolation and only based  on on the current  health crisis obviously you're bound to  have a lot of people circulating uh  and you know with very little capacity  to control them  so there is the the concern the third  concern obviously that  uh you know particularly the mass which  could attract uh  thousands of people this coming sunday  uh in the north of the country could  really become  a super spreader event uh but you know  the vatican takes  the position that is important to be out  there with the people  and this is the way in which they're  trying to fulfill that  what they see as their mission martin  stay with us um the country is a home to  some of the  oldest continuous christian communities  in the world but the  numbers of christians have dropped  dramatically in recent years  before we come back to you martin let's  have a look at their history  in the country vatican and iraqi flags  going up for pope francis's visit  people here are working hard to fix  everything up before he arrives  paving the roads fixing street lights  and setting up crowd control barriers  expectations are high  we hope for this to be a successful  visit and that it will help this  injured country when his holiness the  pope visits any place  there are a lot of positive outcomes  from his visit  church sits on the nineveh plain in iraq  home to some of the earliest christian  settlements  centuries before islam established  itself across the middle east  over time iraq became home to one of the  world's most diverse christian  communities  featuring catholic chaldean armenian  orthodox protestant as well as other  branches of christianity  we are sending a message to the pope and  to the world  that we are here we the christians  are remaining on our land our roots are  here  it is impossible to leave the homeland  that embraced us  there were as many as an estimated 1.4  million christians in the country at the  start of the iraq war  in 2003. sectarian conflict drove most  of them away  the pope's visit is supposed to send a  message of support  but he'll also meet with the head of  iraq's shiite muslim community  sending a different message that the  different faiths can  get along  so martin as we heard the report many  different  variants of christianity there in iraq  what does  what role does the head of the catholic  church really play for  all those different christians in iraq  today  well i mean every one of this every one  of these denominations  has been uh basically under enormous  pressure so i mean on the one hand  even though we can talk about different  communities and different different  denominations  uh you know for uh essentially the  forces that have been impinging  regularly  over the the space of uh of christian  practice in in iraq  uh of course what they see is mostly  just  christians what that means is that the  pope  coming to the area is not really just  sort of something that  will impact sort of the standing of  catholics  in the region or in the country but will  actually go beyond that  and i think that it can be rightly seen  as presenting the case  uh for the christian community as a  whole  i think that for this vatican the  question of inter-religious relations  especially with with the muslim world  has been very very important  um and i think that in the in the  conditions in which a christian  community finds itself this uh  this gains uh obviously new new  relevance  dws religious affairs analyst martin  guck there thank you martin  you
 

